{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RAP7v8YkZozpxZgk1uWH4M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment using Unix utilities as a test bed. It derives formal specs from C source, generates Go implementations from those specs using AI, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/<name>/` - One directory per utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing framework\n- `pkg/sys/` - System call abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `docs/specs/` - SRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - Build automation\n\n**Current State**: Looking at the implementation progress across releases, I see that 00.0 is partially complete with some core infrastructure in place, while most utilities from 01.1 onward have varying levels of completion—some fully implemented like sponge and du, others still in progress like the 03.1 release which appears to be cut off.\n\nNow I'm checking the requirement_states to see which SRDs have their requirements documented, and I notice some key ones are missing from the list—notably testutils, sys, format, and cat—which suggests these might need their requirements defined before implementation can proceed. I'm noticing that some SRDs like srd001-testutils, srd002-sys, and srd003-format aren't showing up in the requirement_states list, which suggests they might already be complete or their requirements aren't being tracked separately. Let me cross-reference the code_status entries to see which ones actually need work versus which are already done. Looking at the constraint more carefully, if a SRD isn't in requirement_states at all, I can't propose tasks for it since there are no \"ready\" R-items to work with. So I need to focus only on the SRDs that are actually tracked and have requirements marked as ready—like srd004-ts, srd008-ls, srd020-echo, srd021-tac, srd022-nl, srd023-fold, and srd024, which all have multiple ready items I can propose tasks for.\n\nNow scanning through the full list of SRDs with all their requirements ready: srd025-unexpand through srd043-groups, plus srd049-real and beyond all have complete ready status. Continuing through the remaining utilities—fmt through nohup and beyond are all ready to go. Looking at the release mapping by code status, most items show all ready with code not started, but the recent commits indicate there's actually active work happening on the ls command that contradicts this status. Looking at the ready requirements across releases, I see srd008-ls has R2.3-R4.9 ready, then several tools become ready in 04.1 and 05.0, with srd004-ts fully ready in release 15.0. By release-driven priority, I should focus on the earliest incomplete release, but I'm realizing I need to check whether tools like srd003 and srd001 have trackable requirements at all before I can propose tasks for them. I'm realizing I don't have the actual weights from requirements.yaml in the project context, and I can't use tools to look it up. The constraint requires checking weights but I'm working with incomplete information—I'll need to assume a conservative default weight of 1 for most requirements unless there's evidence they're heavier, like the weight-4 utilities I can see mentioned for other releases.\n\nSince I'm limited to 4 tasks total and need to stay within the weight constraint, I should aim for roughly 4 requirements per task as a safe baseline. Looking at the ready requirements for srd008-ls, I have a substantial list across the R2 and R3 groups to work with.\n\nThe challenge is that I have 37 ready requirements but can only propose 4 tasks. I need to understand what these requirements actually represent by examining the SRD itself. From the code status, I know ls has two use cases—the core functionality and an extended variant—which likely explains the breadth of requirements. The roadmap mentions \"Full ls\" as the goal, so I should be strategic about which requirements to bundle together into meaningful tasks that align with the project's priorities. I see the issue now—I should just include the SRD as required reading and let the stitch agent handle the details. Rather than trying to guess the requirement structure, I'll organize the ls requirements into batches of four and trust that the agent can reference the SRD to understand what each one covers. Let me group them starting from R2.3 onwards.\n\nSince ls already has existing code from earlier requirements, I don't need a contract task. And looking at the package contracts, all the dependencies are already established packages, so there's nothing new to define there either.\n\nNow I'm mapping out which files these tasks would modify—mainly cmd/ls/main.go and cmd/ls/ls_test.go, though the complexity might mean touching additional files. But I'm noticing a potential issue: ls depends on srd003-format according to the component dependencies, yet format shows as \"not started\" in the code status, which creates a dependency problem since I can't propose tasks for packages that aren't in the requirement states.\n\nHowever, looking at the recent commits, ls requirements R1.1 through R2.2 have already been completed successfully, which suggests either format was created as part of those earlier tasks or ls doesn't actually require it for those specific requirements. The pkg/format/ package likely already exists in some form that the stitch agent will need to read.\n\nI'm going to draft the four tasks for srd008-ls by grouping them sequentially since I can't read the actual SRD to understand what each R-requirement covers—the stitch agent can pull the specific content from the required_reading field.\n\nI need to respect P6's constraint about anchoring tasks to specific SRD requirement IDs and not crossing requirement boundaries, so grouping sequentially within the same requirement group should work fine. I'm also noticing from recent commits that implementation and tests seem to be combined in single tasks rather than split, with tests living alongside the code in files like ls_test.go.\n\nBut P2 is explicit: implementation and tests must be separate tasks. That creates a tension though—if I split them, each task would reference the same 4 requirements, which feels redundant. And with only 4 task slots available, splitting impl and test tasks means I can only cover 2 batches of requirements instead of 4.\n\nLet me think about this differently. I could structure it so one implementation task covers requirements R2.3-R2.6, then a corresponding test task covers the same requirements but focuses on the test implementation. That way I'm respecting the separation rule while still making efficient use of the slots.\n\nLooking at the weight constraints, if each requirement has weight 1, then grouping 4 requirements per task maxes out the weight limit. But I should verify whether the ls requirements actually have uniform weights—some might be heavier, especially if they're part of extended features rather than core functionality. Without access to the actual SRD, I'll assume conservatively that weights could vary.\n\nActually, let me just move forward with 4 tasks of at most 4 R-items each, assuming weight 1 per requirement. If the orchestrator finds any weight violations, it can re-run. For the task titles, I'll follow the pattern from recent commits like \"cmd/ls: remaining core and extended column layout (srd008-ls R1.13-R1.14, R2.1-R2.2)\".\n\nSo I'm drafting: Task 0 handles sort and metadata features for R2.3-R2.6 with implementation in main.go, Task 1 covers the corresponding tests depending on Task 0, and Task 2 will handle the extended features.\n\nWait, I'm realizing the core issue here—the stitch agent won't know what R2.3 through R2.10 actually entail since I can't access the SRD directly. The right approach is to reference the SRD as required_reading and let the agent pull the actual requirement details from there, then map them to the R-items in the task description.\n\nFor the task titles, I should probably be more specific about which requirement group I'm covering rather than using vague names. Looking at the structure, I've got R2 through R4 groups with varying numbers of items, and the roadmap hints at what features fall into the extended category—things like the -C and -x flags, sorting options, metadata display, classification, and recursion. Since I can't read the SRD myself, I'll need to reference the specific R-items and let the agent figure out the details.\n\nNow I'm mapping out how to split this work. The R2 group likely covers extended column layout and sorting, R3 probably handles metadata and classification features, and R4 deals with error handling. So I'll create four tasks: implementation and tests for the sort/display features covering R2.3-R2.6, then implementation and tests for the metadata features covering R2.7-R2.10.\n\nLooking at the actual commit titles though, I see the pattern is `[stitch] cmd/ls: <description> (srd008-ls <R-items>)`, and each task bundles about 4 R-items together. Let me align my task breakdown with that structure.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether to keep implementation and tests separate. P2 is pretty clear that a single task shouldn't do both, but it also says \"up to three tasks\" and allows combining steps 1 and 2 if they fit the line budget. Since cmd/ls already exists and I'm just adding more requirements, I could reasonably combine the implementation and tests into one task per batch of R-items, or keep them separate. Let me think through what makes sense given the existing pattern in the codebase. tasks modify cmd/ls/ls_test.go and depend on their corresponding implementation tasks. I need to figure out the required reading and files for each task - the SRD, existing implementation in main.go, architecture dependencies in pkg/sys/ and pkg/format/, and the differential testing pattern in pkg/testutils/. Let me start drafting the actual task descriptions as YAML documents, with implementation tasks modifying main.go and test tasks modifying ls_test.go.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering whether to split implementation and tests into separate tasks. Looking at recent commit patterns and the generation pipeline, it seems like combined tasks are expected. The constraint about 4 R-items per task with weight 4 max suggests each task should cover a small set of requirements fully - both implementation and tests together. Splitting them would create dependencies where test tasks can't be verified without the implementation task completing first, which violates the independence requirement. A combined task where the agent implements R2.3-R2.6 in main.go and adds corresponding test cases in ls_test.go is a natural, independently executable unit of work.\n\nBut wait - P2 explicitly states that a single task must never both implement and test the same feature. That's a firm rule I need to follow. So I'm going with 2 implementation tasks and 2 test tasks instead, even though it means lower throughput on the R-item coverage.\n\nNow I'm working through the dependency structure. Both task 0 and task 2 modify main.go, so task 2 needs to depend on task 0 to avoid conflicts when they run in parallel. Task 1 depends on task 0 since tests need the implementation first, and task 3 depends on task 2 for the same reason. I'm realizing that task 3 needs to wait for both tasks 1 and 2 since they both modify ls_test.go, but the schema only allows a single dependency. The safest approach is to chain them sequentially: 0 → 1 → 2 → 3, so task 3 depends on task 2, which depends on task 1, which depends on task 0. Sequential chain is cleaner and avoids unnecessary delays. Now I'm mapping out descriptive names for the task groups based on the roadmap features—extended column and sort operations for one group, metadata and classification for another, and error handling for the third. I'll use the R-group numbers as a basis for clear, generic titles and then format everything properly in YAML.\n\nActually, I should reconsider whether P2's split rule applies here. Looking at the commit history, the existing pattern for cmd/ utilities combines implementation and tests in single tasks, not separate ones. Even though P2 suggests splitting them, the project convention and the differential testing approach for command packages means keeping them together makes more sense. I'll go with combined tasks to match what's already established.\n\nNow I'm sketching out how to organize the extended features across multiple tasks, breaking them down by requirement ranges to keep each task focused and manageable.\n\nBut wait—the constraint is absolute: I can't have a single task doing both implementation and testing. I need to split them into separate tasks entirely. So with four slots available, I should allocate two for implementation work and two for testing, each covering the same set of requirements in sequence.\n\nLet me map this out: Task 0 handles implementing R2.3 through R2.6, Task 1 tests those same items, Task 2 implements R2.7 through R2.10, and Task 3 tests that second batch. This way I'm respecting the four-requirement limit per task while maintaining the strict separation between implementation and testing phases.\n\nNow I need to structure the YAML with proper deliverable types, required reading, file modifications, and acceptance criteria. I'm laying out the design decisions for this task—extending the existing ls implementation without creating new files, leveraging the sys and format packages, and using the LC_ALL=C pattern for testing. The acceptance criteria cover all four requirements R2.3 through R2.6 to ensure the implementation is complete and correct.\n\nNow I'm realizing the requirement text is too vague—just saying \"Implement srd008-ls R2.3 per the SRD specification\" doesn't capture what R2.3 actually does. Since I can't read the SRD directly, I'll need to reference the R-item IDs themselves, and the stitch agent can look up the specifics. The constraint is clear that each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task requirement, so I shouldn't split them up.\n\nFor the task titles, I should follow the pattern from the commit messages like \"cmd/ls: core display features (srd008-ls R1.9-R1.12)\". Without knowing what R2.3-R2.6 cover, I'll keep it descriptive but general. Actually, the key insight is that the task description needs to be self-contained for the stitch agent—it reads the SRD from the required_reading section, so I should write requirements that tell it to implement specific R-items, and it'll fetch the details from there.\n\nA good task description needs to tell the agent where to make changes in the codebase, what patterns to follow, and which SRD requirements to anchor to. Looking at the output format example, the requirements text is pretty concise—something like \"Implement CrumbTable interface per srd003-crumbs-interface\" works well. So I should be brief and practical: specify the files to modify, reference the SRD R-items, and mention any design constraints or verification steps.\n\nFor the ls package, I'm assuming there might be multiple source files beyond main.go since complex utilities often split into helpers for sorting, formatting, and columns. I'll note that the stitch agent should read all existing files in cmd/ls/ and modify as needed. And I need to follow the constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks—so this task should focus on implementation only, with tests coming later.\n\nNow I'm working through the task decomposition with the weight constraints. I'll split the requirements into two groups: R2.3-R2.6 for the first implementation task and R2.7-R2.10 for the second, each with